External vendor APIs fail — network timeouts, rate limits, transient 500 errors. The retry strategy must handle these without creating duplicate notifications or blocking the queue.

**Exponential backoff with jitter** is the standard retry pattern. First retry after 1 second, then 2, 4, 8, with random jitter to prevent thundering herd when multiple notifications retry simultaneously. Cap at 5 retries for transient failures.

The retry implementation distinguishes between **retryable** and **non-retryable** failures. A 429 (rate limited) or 503 (service unavailable) is retryable. A 400 (bad request — invalid email address) is not — retrying will never succeed. Non-retryable failures go directly to a **dead letter queue** (DLQ) for manual review.

```java
@Retryable(maxAttempts = 5,
    backoff = @Backoff(delay = 1000, multiplier = 2, maxDelay = 30000),
    include = {RetryableException.class})
public void sendEmail(NotificationRequest request) {
    try {
        sendGridClient.send(request.toEmail());
    } catch (RateLimitException e) {
        throw new RetryableException(e);     // will retry
    } catch (InvalidRecipientException e) {
        dlqPublisher.send(request);           // permanent failure
        throw new NonRetryableException(e);  // won't retry
    }
}
```

The DLQ is monitored with alerts — more than 100 messages in 1 hour triggers investigation. A separate reconciliation job periodically replays DLQ messages after the root cause is fixed.
